1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,892
In my own team, some people don't yet

2
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:07,272
trust AI enough to use it more.

3
00:00:07,865 --> 00:00:10,663
Others are already using it enthusiastically.

4
00:00:11,766 --> 00:00:13,681
I am closer to that second group.

5
00:00:14,633 --> 00:00:18,070
Listening to DHH talk with Lex Fridman, I

6
00:00:18,256 --> 00:00:20,322
recognized that excitement.

7
00:00:20,914 --> 00:00:22,760
Three ideas stayed with me.

8
00:00:23,445 --> 00:00:26,290
Together, they helped me explain why I want

9
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:29,668
to give AI more responsibility, and what I

10
00:00:29,784 --> 00:00:32,536
want that to mean for our work.

11
00:00:32,536 --> 00:00:34,823
First, the tool can change enough to deserve

12
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:36,100
a fresh judgment.

13
00:00:37,261 --> 00:00:40,826
DHH describes a gap between earlier autocomplete and

14
00:00:40,918 --> 00:00:44,610
agents that could use tools, check their work,

15
00:00:45,226 --> 00:00:48,615
and deliver code much closer to his expectations.

16
00:00:49,776 --> 00:00:52,714
His explanation is that his standards remained; the

17
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,002
experience changed.

18
00:00:55,117 --> 00:00:56,998
I find that attitude useful.

19
00:00:57,694 --> 00:01:00,167
If I tried something and it disappointed me,

20
00:01:00,272 --> 00:01:01,862
that experience matters.

21
00:01:02,466 --> 00:01:04,417
But I want to remember what I actually

22
00:01:04,452 --> 00:01:05,021
tested.

23
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:08,539
A conclusion from that attempt has a scope.

24
00:01:09,583 --> 00:01:11,894
The same applies to a good experience.

25
00:01:13,113 --> 00:01:15,563
I can be excited by a result without

26
00:01:15,679 --> 00:01:18,303
assuming every task is now solved.

27
00:01:19,185 --> 00:01:21,368
What I take from him is a willingness

28
00:01:21,472 --> 00:01:24,967
to return to the work and look again.

29
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:29,134
Second, being able to implement something makes product

30
00:01:29,215 --> 00:01:30,968
judgment more important.

31
00:01:31,642 --> 00:01:35,984
DHH emphasizes decisions about the people software serves,

32
00:01:36,413 --> 00:01:40,639
its behavior, and its priorities when agents handle

33
00:01:40,686 --> 00:01:41,696
implementation.

34
00:01:42,369 --> 00:01:45,318
That connects directly with what I care about.

35
00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:49,521
Imagine a small improvement to a business workflow.

36
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:52,911
Before we build anything, someone has to notice

37
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:53,735
the friction.

38
00:01:54,792 --> 00:01:58,240
Someone has to understand why the same information

39
00:01:58,321 --> 00:02:03,162
gets entered twice, or why a perfectly functional

40
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,206
screen leaves people confused.

41
00:02:06,192 --> 00:02:08,398
If AI gives me more room to explore

42
00:02:08,445 --> 00:02:11,463
an idea, I want to use that room

43
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:13,843
to understand the person who might use it.

44
00:02:14,830 --> 00:02:17,106
Producing more options only helps if we can

45
00:02:17,153 --> 00:02:19,533
choose something useful.

46
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:22,772
Third, the joy of creating can survive a

47
00:02:22,830 --> 00:02:24,281
change in how we work.

48
00:02:25,048 --> 00:02:30,133
When Lex asks about programmers' anxiety, DHH distinguishes

49
00:02:30,226 --> 00:02:34,765
enthusiasm for building things from attachment to programming's

50
00:02:34,928 --> 00:02:36,263
mechanical tasks.

51
00:02:36,913 --> 00:02:40,024
He also acknowledges uncertainty about employment.

52
00:02:41,232 --> 00:02:44,169
I hear an optimistic view of creating, without

53
00:02:44,192 --> 00:02:47,164
treating it as a promise about anyone's job.

54
00:02:48,209 --> 00:02:51,158
My own excitement comes from seeing an idea

55
00:02:51,228 --> 00:02:52,296
become useful.

56
00:02:53,051 --> 00:02:54,792
Somebody can get on with the part of

57
00:02:54,885 --> 00:02:56,882
their day they actually care about.

58
00:02:57,997 --> 00:03:01,875
I also admire the ambition behind Omarchy: taking

59
00:03:01,921 --> 00:03:05,926
strong preferences about a working environment and building

60
00:03:05,996 --> 00:03:08,016
a whole project around them.

61
00:03:09,131 --> 00:03:11,523
That impulse feels familiar to me.

62
00:03:12,637 --> 00:03:15,017
I want more worthwhile ideas to get a

63
00:03:15,087 --> 00:03:17,200
chance to exist.

64
00:03:20,551 --> 00:03:23,047
Those three ideas come together in my blog

65
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:23,837
workflow.

66
00:03:24,638 --> 00:03:27,761
An idea can move through research, an outline,

67
00:03:27,865 --> 00:03:31,093
a draft, bilingual adaptation, and review.

68
00:03:32,184 --> 00:03:35,203
Each step has reusable guidance, and there are

69
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:37,478
files I can inspect and revise.

70
00:03:38,024 --> 00:03:39,730
This piece is an example.

71
00:03:40,369 --> 00:03:42,563
I wanted to express why I agreed with

72
00:03:42,598 --> 00:03:43,411
DHH.

73
00:03:44,282 --> 00:03:46,545
An earlier draft gave too much weight to

74
00:03:46,662 --> 00:03:48,252
my interest in Linux.

75
00:03:49,135 --> 00:03:51,398
I asked for the emphasis to move back

76
00:03:51,456 --> 00:03:54,336
to AI, and to the different levels of

77
00:03:54,417 --> 00:03:55,845
trust in my own team.

78
00:03:56,541 --> 00:03:59,723
The draft helped make that mismatch visible.

79
00:04:00,419 --> 00:04:02,683
I could point to what felt wrong and

80
00:04:02,764 --> 00:04:04,993
explain what mattered more.

81
00:04:05,690 --> 00:04:08,360
That is a kind of collaboration I value:

82
00:04:08,871 --> 00:04:11,820
moving an idea forward while I keep shaping

83
00:04:11,901 --> 00:04:13,353
its meaning.

84
00:04:13,353 --> 00:04:16,267
Inside a company, there is another challenge.

85
00:04:17,126 --> 00:04:20,679
My confidence does not automatically become somebody else's

86
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:21,584
confidence.

87
00:04:22,757 --> 00:04:25,660
I have colleagues who are actively using AI,

88
00:04:25,915 --> 00:04:28,689
and others who don't yet trust it enough.

89
00:04:29,862 --> 00:04:32,567
I don't want to turn DHH's enthusiasm into

90
00:04:32,683 --> 00:04:35,493
an argument that they should simply agree with

91
00:04:35,563 --> 00:04:35,899
me.

92
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:39,835
His experience gives us a useful reference.

93
00:04:40,485 --> 00:04:43,132
Our project still needs its own evidence.

94
00:04:44,166 --> 00:04:46,697
I would like the people using AI successfully

95
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:49,367
to make their methods easier to inspect.

96
00:04:50,064 --> 00:04:53,767
And I want reservations to become specific questions

97
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:55,900
we can investigate.

98
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:01,002
Here is a small exercise I would like

99
00:05:01,072 --> 00:05:02,035
us to try.

100
00:05:03,196 --> 00:05:05,425
Take a paragraph based on an interview.

101
00:05:06,273 --> 00:05:09,257
Ask AI to make it clearer while preserving

102
00:05:09,303 --> 00:05:09,907
the meaning.

103
00:05:11,068 --> 00:05:14,365
Before we start, agree that a useful result

104
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:18,441
must be readable, match the source, and keep

105
00:05:18,522 --> 00:05:22,365
personal interpretations separate from factual claims.

106
00:05:22,945 --> 00:05:25,825
The person using AI can show the instructions,

107
00:05:25,987 --> 00:05:29,784
the source material, and the changes they accepted.

108
00:05:30,364 --> 00:05:33,859
A colleague can then examine the result against

109
00:05:33,951 --> 00:05:36,308
those same expectations.

110
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:39,664
That makes the discussion concrete.

111
00:05:40,616 --> 00:05:44,133
We can ask which edit improved the paragraph,

112
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:48,139
which one needs checking, and whether the method

113
00:05:48,208 --> 00:05:50,380
is worth repeating.

114
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:53,306
Consider a possible mistake in that exercise.

115
00:05:54,548 --> 00:05:57,578
DHH describes agents doing useful work.

116
00:05:58,159 --> 00:06:00,423
That is a statement about the interview.

117
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,314
More of my ideas may now be worth

118
00:06:03,348 --> 00:06:03,894
trying.

119
00:06:04,207 --> 00:06:05,879
That is my interpretation.

120
00:06:06,146 --> 00:06:07,876
Both can belong in a draft.

121
00:06:08,968 --> 00:06:11,557
But if a rewrite turns them into everyone

122
00:06:11,603 --> 00:06:14,854
is more productive now, it has added a

123
00:06:14,923 --> 00:06:16,793
claim we haven't established.

124
00:06:17,884 --> 00:06:21,007
A confident sentence can sound better while becoming

125
00:06:21,077 --> 00:06:22,180
less accurate.

126
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:25,709
The enthusiastic person can show where the tool

127
00:06:25,790 --> 00:06:26,406
helped.

128
00:06:26,917 --> 00:06:29,680
The person with reservations can point to the

129
00:06:29,749 --> 00:06:31,096
claim that drifted.

130
00:06:32,048 --> 00:06:35,369
If the paragraph improved, keep the method.

131
00:06:35,972 --> 00:06:39,316
If it failed, adjust the instructions or review

132
00:06:39,397 --> 00:06:39,966
step.

133
00:06:40,918 --> 00:06:42,973
This is a practice I want to try,

134
00:06:43,089 --> 00:06:45,585
rather than a story about a team problem

135
00:06:45,702 --> 00:06:48,151
we have already solved.

136
00:06:48,151 --> 00:06:50,821
A writing exercise would teach us about that

137
00:06:50,879 --> 00:06:51,541
task.

138
00:06:52,609 --> 00:06:55,825
Moving into a shared business system raises different

139
00:06:55,895 --> 00:06:56,812
questions.

140
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:00,632
I have written before about QA pressure when

141
00:07:00,701 --> 00:07:03,952
changes arrive faster than people can understand and

142
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:04,974
verify them.

143
00:07:06,158 --> 00:07:08,468
More output can move the work into someone

144
00:07:08,526 --> 00:07:09,478
else's queue.

145
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:12,125
That is part of delivery, too.

146
00:07:13,147 --> 00:07:15,899
I still want to delegate more to AI.

147
00:07:16,642 --> 00:07:18,383
I also want to know who depends on

148
00:07:18,406 --> 00:07:21,402
the result, how we will notice a failure,

149
00:07:21,495 --> 00:07:23,108
and how we can recover.

150
00:07:24,223 --> 00:07:27,183
Trust can grow as we gain experience.

151
00:07:28,484 --> 00:07:30,875
The evidence we ask for should fit the

152
00:07:30,933 --> 00:07:34,730
consequences of getting something wrong.

153
00:07:34,730 --> 00:07:37,853
For my team, I would like that enthusiasm

154
00:07:37,911 --> 00:07:40,222
to lead to a shared piece of work.

155
00:07:41,266 --> 00:07:42,927
We don't all have to start with the

156
00:07:42,985 --> 00:07:44,169
same confidence.

157
00:07:45,063 --> 00:07:48,174
We can start with a familiar task, agree

158
00:07:48,221 --> 00:07:50,845
on what good looks like, and inspect the

159
00:07:50,915 --> 00:07:52,006
result together.

160
00:07:53,121 --> 00:07:55,872
If we finish with one method worth keeping,

161
00:07:56,383 --> 00:07:58,194
we have something we can use the next

162
00:07:58,275 --> 00:07:59,065
morning.

163
00:08:03,015 --> 00:08:05,546
So what I take from DHH is this:

164
00:08:05,941 --> 00:08:09,412
stay curious as the tools change, be thoughtful

165
00:08:09,482 --> 00:08:12,129
about what we build, and keep the joy

166
00:08:12,245 --> 00:08:13,185
of creating.

167
00:08:14,195 --> 00:08:16,773
That is why I share his enthusiasm for

168
00:08:16,831 --> 00:08:17,446
AI.

169
00:08:17,853 --> 00:08:20,406
I hope we can turn that enthusiasm into

170
00:08:20,499 --> 00:08:24,609
trust we earn together, and products that make

171
00:08:24,644 --> 00:08:26,792
someone's day a little better.
